    "OtherAbstract": {
      "Type": "PIP",
      "Language": "eng",
      "AbstractText": "To determine if knowledge of endometrial cavity length is helpful in the prediction of IUD performance, the Wing Sound was used to determine the length of the cervical canal and of the uterine cavity. devices used were the Dalkon Shield, 20 mm long, Lippes Loops, 28 mm, and copper-7, 36 mm. IUDs were inserted above the level of the internal cervical os and selected according to the length of the endometrial cavity. In some cases the length of the endometrial cavity was shorter than the IUD. 319 patients were followed for 9 or more months. The mean duration of use was 18.2 months. events considered were pregnancy, expulsion, and medical removals. Differences between the length of the endometrial cavity and the length of the IUD was associated with different (p less than .01) event rates. An event rate of 48.9% was noted when the length of the device was equal to or exceeded the endometrial length. The lowest event rates were found as the endometrial length became greater than the IUD length but the highest event rate was noted in patients whose endometrial cavity was 2 or more cm longer than the IUD. Patients with shorter cavity lengths were more likely to have the IUD removed because of bleeding or pain. Devices that occupied the lower segment and the isthmus had high explusion or medical removal rates and possible pregnancy if the upper segment was not covered by the IUD. devices that protruded into the cervical canal were more liable to expulsion. This study shows the value of selecting IUD size on the basis of endometrial cavity length. Total uterine length is not suitable for estimating endometrial cavity length. With the Wing Sound the desired measurement can be obtained."
    }
